A||N||7||reddog||water in fuel filter||||1||
Z||000000||reddog||10-09-2001||08:22 PM||||I recently bought a new fuel filter for my GT but have not installed it yet. I left the filter in the trunk in its package for about a week. Yesterday I removed the filter and like most fieros, I discovered that the trunk weather strip was leaking and the entire cardboard backing on the filter package was soaked. There also was some condensation inside the package. Can this fuel filter still be dried out and used? Or should I just toss it and get a new one?||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfiero||10-09-2001||08:49 PM||||Since they're less than $10, I would just buy another one.<P>You could flush it out with some gas to get whatever water might be inside.....it's up to you (but I would just buy a new one).<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pheagey||10-09-2001||09:59 PM||||Take it from me dude even a little water is bad in a 4 (or 6) banger. Trash the filter and get a new one. I recommende anything by FRAM.<BR> <A HREF="Http://www.LithiumEffects.com/car.html" TARGET=_blank>Http://www.LithiumEffects.com/car.html</A> ||||reg||2
Z||000003||Pheagey||10-09-2001||09:59 PM||||My bad-double post (d@mn slow machine). <p>[This message has been edited by Pheagey (edited 10-09-2001).]||||reg||2
Z||000004||theogre||10-10-2001||08:01 PM||||Unless you filled it with water, I doubt you hurt it.... I have a Fram one in my hand, didn't get a chance to put it on yet... Just stick it someplace warm to evaporate the water before installing it. (Don't roast it...) The filter is built to tolerate water in the fuel. Part of it's job is to provide a gravity trap for small amounts of water that the pump might pickup.<P>Make sure you install them correctly. One end is stamped as "outlet." You can read that even if the writing on the side gets errased.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||California Kid||10-10-2001||08:46 PM||||There's only two safe options: 1. Buy a new filter. 2. Buy a small can of "Dry Gas" and let the filter set submerged in it overnight, shake out and install.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature_7.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||SLYGUY||10-11-2001||03:54 AM||||I've been meaning to change the fuel filter in my GT but I'm not sure where it is at. In the Haynes manual you get a nice black and wh<BR>ite picture of the filter, but no description of where it is at... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> <P>Thanks again for your help...<P>Sly||||reg||1
